    "query": "China industrial production exports GDP slowdown Q1 2019 trade war impact",
    "search_window": {
      "from": "2018-10-05",
      "to": "2019-04-03"
    },
    "sources": {
      "grounding": {
        "generic": [
          {
            "url": "https://www.reuters.com/article/us-china-economy-activity/china-industrial-output-growth-falls-to-17-year-low-more-support-steps-expected-idUSKCN1QV058/",
            "title": "China industrial output growth falls to 17-year low, more support ...",
            "snippets": [
              "# China industrial output growth falls to 17-year low, more support steps expected\nBEIJING (Reuters) - Growth in China's industrial output fell to a 17-year low in the first two months of the year and the jobless rate rose, pointing to further weakness in the world's second-biggest economy that is likely to trigger more support measures from Beijing.\n\nBut a mixed bag of major data on Thursday also showed property investment was picking up, while overall retail sales were sluggish but steady, suggesting the economy is not in the midst of a sharper slowdown",
              "China is ramping up assistance for the economy as 2019 growth looks set to plumb 29-year lows, but support measures are taking time to kick in. Most analysts believe activity may not convincingly stabilise until the middle of the year",
              "Pressured by weak demand at home and abroad, China's industrial output rose 5.3 percent in January-February, less than expected and the slowest pace since early 2002. Growth had been expected to cool to 5.5 percent from December's 5.7 percent",
              "China combines January and February activity data in an attempt to smooth distortions created by the long Lunar New Year holidays early each year, but some analysts say a clearer picture of the economy's health may not emerge until first-quarter data is released in April.\n\nIf the seasonal distortion was removed, output rose 6.1 percent in the two months, the National Bureau of Statistics said",
              "China's own official factory survey, which is seasonally adjusted, showed manufacturing output contracted in February for the first time since January 2009.\n\nData last week showed exports tumbled the most in three years in February, suggesting U.S. tariffs on Chinese goods and cooling global demand were taking a greater toll",
              "China's survey-based jobless rate rose to 5.3 percent in February, from 4.9 percent in December, though it was below the government's target of 5.5 percent this year.\n\nMany migrant workers also quit their jobs to go home before the holidays, Li said.\n\nReuters reported in January that some factories in Guangdong - China's export hub - had shut earlier than usual ahead of the holidays, and some were expected to close for good as the trade war curtailed orders"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://finance.yahoo.com/news/china-industrial-output-growth-falls-055550834.html",
            "title": "China industrial output growth falls to 17-yr low, more support ...",
            "snippets": [
              "# China industrial output growth falls to 17-yr low, more support steps expected\n(Adds details on jump in unemployment)\n\n* Mixed data point to further economic slowdown as expected\n\n* But no signs of sharp loss of momentum yet\n\n* Jan-Feb fixed-asset investment +6.1 pct y/y (poll +6.0 pct)\n\n* Jan-Feb industrial output +5.3 pct y/y (poll +5.5 pct)",
              "By Kevin Yao and Stella Qiu\n\nBEIJING, March 14 (Reuters) - Growth in China's industrial output fell to a 17-year low in the first two months of the year and the jobless rate rose, pointing to further weakness in the world's second-biggest economy that is likely to trigger more support measures from Beijing",
              "But a mixed bag of major data on Thursday also showed property investment was picking up, while overall retail sales were sluggish but steady, suggesting the economy is not in the midst of a sharper slowdown.\n\nChina is ramping up assistance for the economy as 2019 growth looks set to plumb 29-year lows, but support measures are taking time to kick in. Most analysts believe activity may not convincingly stabilise until the middle of the year",
              "Premier Li Keqiang last week announced hundreds of billions of dollars in additional tax cuts and infrastructure spending, even as officials vowed they would not resort to massive stimulus like in the past, which produced swift recoveries in China and strong reflationary pulses worldwide.\n\n\"The latest data should partially ease concerns about a sharp slowdown at the start of the year. But the near-term outlook still looks downbeat,\" Capital Economics said in a note",
              "Reuters reported in January that some factories in Guangdong - China's export hub - had shut earlier than usual ahead of the holidays, and some were expected to close for good as the trade war curtailed orders.\n\nINVESTMENT PICKING UP"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.forbes.com/sites/kenrapoza/2018/12/17/chinas-new-growing-pains-not-all-because-of-trade-war/",
            "title": "China's New Growing Pains Not All Due To Trump's Trade War",
            "snippets": [
              "# China's New Growing Pains Not All Due To Trump's Trade War\nChina’s economy is going through a serious structural change. It’s changing the face of manufacturing and labor markets there at a time when President Trump is waging a trade war against China. Despite those tougher conditions for Chinese exports, many of which are made as contractors building and assembling for American brands, the China slowdown has more to do with a quickly modernizing economy than weakness in global trade",
              "In the simplest of terms, China is automating its factories. It is the largest buyer of robotic equipment. Their companies are setting up shop in Southeast Asian nations like Vietnam, building factories or contracting manufacturers abroad to do their work. It’s like old United States mill towns in the 1970s and 1980s who relocated first to cheaper producers in southern states, then later to China.",
              "ZTO Express workers prepare packages at a sorting facility in Shanghai. E-commerce is one of China’s leading industries and much of it is being automated. China’s economy is becoming less of a manufacturing one and more like the United States centered on domestic consumption.",
              "The economy is still growing, however. Third-quarter growth was—you guessed it—6.5%. That is right within Beijing’s forecast, but is down from 6.7% in the second quarter and below market consensus forecasts.\n\nIndustrial production grew 5.4% versus an estimate of 5.9% and October’s 5.9% growth rate. Fixed-asset investment in things like roads and bridges rose 5.9% versus an estimate of 5.8% and October’s 5.7%",
              "“The biggest surprise to me is that investors took the news so badly,” says Brendan Ahern, CIO of KraneShares. “Did anyone think it was going to be good? The market appeared surprised as evidenced by Friday’s poor equity stock performance, which one mainland broker called ‘tragic.’ December and potentially first-quarter economic data is going to get worse,” Ahern wrote in a note to clients on Friday.",
              "In Saturday’s *New York Times* print version, reporters Keith Bradsher and Ailan Tang delved a little deeper into what’s going on with China’s manufacturing sector. The picture they offer appears to be a manufacturing recession serious enough to prompt many of China’s roughly 270 million migrant workers to head back to the hinterlands as companies reduce hours and start laying off workers",
              "China’s manufacturing is not in a crisis. It is, instead, at a crossroads.\n\nSome of China’s manufacturers are in trouble because of bad debt and bad business management. Others are in trouble because they are export-dependent. And some are doing quite well, thanks to domestic demand.\n\nThe upside to China’s structural shift, and its manufacturing slowdown, is that some 78% of China’s GDP is now coming from the consumer. Chinese still saves about a third of their income. There is more money to be spent",
              "“The domestic economy is strong, so long as you are holistic about China,” says Andy Rothman, an investment strategist for Matthews Asia. “Growth has been slowing on-trend, and the government is fine with that.”",
              "According to the *NYT* article, some economists looking at China from abroad say the slowdown is the worst since the global financial crisis of 2008-2009. Back then, China pumped over $2 trillion into its economy to keep out of a recession seeing how its number one trading partner was eyeballs deep in one",
              "Pedestrians wearing face masks walk across a street as buildings stand shrouded in haze in Beijing on November 14, 2018. China has cracked down on polluting industries, especially coal and metals producers. That’s hurt some old manufacturers, and it is unclear how many of them have folded. Photographer: Giulia Marchi/Bloomberg\n\n© 2018 Bloomberg Finance LP\n\nChina is now stimulating its economy again as manufacturing slows along with the general economy.",
              "“This is not going to be the usual Chinese stimulus,” says Janet Mui, an economist with Cazenove Capital.\n\nChina has ramped up the sort of government-led spending that bailed out its economy in the past, the *NYT* reported. They mention one Xuzhou Construction Machinery Group, a multinational state-owned company whose sales rose 50% from a year ago thanks to Beijing"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2019/jan/14/china-trade-slowdown-exports-us-surplus-eurozone-industrial-production-business-live",
            "title": "Recession fears grow as eurozone factories stumble and China's ...",
            "snippets": [
              "# Recession fears grow as eurozone factories stumble and China's exports fall - as it happened\nThis article is more than 7 years old\n\nStock markets are down again as weak eurozone factory output and Chinese trade woes worry investors\n\n- Latest: Eurozone industrial production shrank 1.7% in November\n- Worst decline in almost three years.\n- Introduction: China’s exports fell 4.4% in December\n- Biggest fall in exports in two years as slowdown gathers pace\n- But US-China trade surplus hits a record\n\nUpdated 14 Jan 2019\n\nGraeme Wearden",
              "- 14 Jan 2019 China-US trade surplus hits record high\n- 14 Jan 2019 China's December trade figures worry markets",
